    "readout_text": "To solve the problem, we begin by analyzing the total amount of orange juice and how it should be distributed among the four glasses.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Determine the Total Amount of Juice\n\nEach glass has a capacity of 1 unit. Mrs. Jones fills the first three glasses completely, so that's:\n\n$$\n3 \\times 1 = 3 \\text{ units}\n$$\n\nShe fills the fourth glass only $\\frac{1}{3}$ full, so that's:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3} \\text{ units}\n$$\n\nThus, the total amount of juice is:\n\n$$\n3 + \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{10}{3} \\text{ units}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Determine the Equal Amount per Glass\n\nSince there are 4 glasses, and the juice is to be distributed equally, each glass should have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{3} \\div 4 = \\frac{10}{12} = \\frac{5}{6} \\text{ units}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Determine How Much to Transfer\n\nEach of the first three glasses currently has 1 unit. To make them each have $\\frac{5}{6}$ units, we need to remove:\n\n$$\n1 - \\frac{5}{6} = \\frac{1}{6} \\text{ units from each of the first three glasses}\n$$\n\nSo, Mrs. Jones must pour out $\\frac{1}{6}$ of a glass from each of the first three glasses.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Verify the Fourth Glass\n\nThe fourth glass currently has $\\frac{1}{3}$ units. If we pour $\\frac{1}{6}$ from each of the three glasses into it, the total amount poured into the fourth glass is:\n\n$$\n3 \\times \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{1}{2} \\text{ units}\n$$\n\nAdding this to the original $\\frac{1}{3}$ in the fourth glass:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{2}{6} + \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{5}{6} \\text{ units}\n$$\n\nThis confirms that all four glasses will have $\\frac{5}{6}$ units of juice, as desired.\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\nThe fraction of a glass that must be poured from each of the first three glasses into the fourth is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, $m = 1$, $n = 6$, and $m + n = 7$.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{7}\n$$",
